Z||000008||Bob Englert||03-30-2001||11:23 PM||||I think you are confusing the 4 cyl and v6 motors.  The 4 cyl 2.5L cars used a Phenolic timing gear that has a reputation for coming apart without any warning.  I suspect quite a few 4 cyl Fieros in junk yards were put there by the timing gear.<P>The 2.8L V6 motors used a standard timing chain set.  This is an all metal set that did not have any real history of breakage as far as I know.  They do stretch with time and mileage and occassionally they can jump a tooth which will cause problems.<P>I think most v6 owners would run the car until a problem occurs, which would probably mean time for a rebuild.  <P>I would personally recommend that any 4 cyl owner with more than 100k consider replacing the phenolic timing gear with a replacement metal gear.  I did this on my first 85 SE (now sold) and will have the metal gear installed when I have the engine rebuilt in my current 85 SE (patiently waiting for future resoration).<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 03-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jules||03-31-2001||01:26 AM||||The 2.8L V6 motors used a standard timing chain set.  This is an all metal set that did not have any real history of breakage as far as I know.  They do stretch with time and mileage and occassionally they can jump a tooth which will cause problems.<P>So this generally doesn't need replaced? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||03-31-2001||01:40 AM||||Generally, if the timing gears and chain are wore out, everything else in the engine is trashed too.||||reg||1
